WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.109
The next topology we are going to cover is a ring topology. This is another topology you don't see very often in a LAN.

00:07.109 --> 00:17.334
One ring topology, Token Ring, was popular, oh, about 20 years ago but since then, the only ring networks commonly in use are Fiber Distributed Data Interface,

00:17.334 --> 00:22.107
or FDDI networks, to where fiber optic cable connects networks within a ring.

00:22.107 --> 00:30.904
In a ring topology, data transmissions are token-based, meaning only one token of data is being passed at a time from device to device.

00:30.904 --> 00:39.800
The positive is that this helps a network to avoid having collisions. The negative is that if there is a break in the ring, the network is down.

00:39.800 --> 00:51.697
For that reason, FDDI networks often have two rings. Both rings are active, but, if one breaks, that is OK as the other ring will take up the workload until the broken ring is fixed.

00:51.697 --> 00:58.872
Another network topology that was once very popular but is not used nearly as much now is a bus topology.

00:58.872 --> 01:07.906
In a bus topology, every device on a network is connected to a backbone cable that runs from one end of the network to the other.

01:07.906 --> 01:12.127
This backbone cable needs terminators at both ends of the cable so that signals,

01:12.127 --> 01:17.838
as they travel from one device to another along the bus, can bounce off the end of the cable if needed.

01:17.838 --> 01:22.535
Without these terminators, signals do not bounce and we do not have a functional network.

01:22.535 --> 01:30.781
Bus networks are also best kept small in size, because any break in the bus means the entire network is down.

01:30.781 --> 01:36.920
And, this includes taking down the network to add or remove a device from the network.

